WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.380
Hi, my name is Simone Tong.

00:00:11.389 --> 00:00:14.200
I'm the chef at Little Tong Noodle Shop.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.250
This restaurant is dedicated to mixian noodles.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:22.750
They're rice noodles that originated from
Yunnan Province in the southwest part of China.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:30.070
Yunnan borders Myanmar, Vietnam, and Laos,
so there's a lot of Southeast Asian influence

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.390
also.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:33.830
In New York, there are many different kinds
of noodle shops.

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:38.870
We have soba, we have ramen, and we have pho,
which is one of my favorites.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.800
Pho is made of rice and so is mixian.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:48.470
What makes this noodle different from pho—or
other noodles—is the broth, and the ingredients

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:50.840
that we bring together with the mixian.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.470
The texture has a little more of a spaghetti
feel, but it's all made out of rice, so you

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.700
don't feel very full after eating it.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:05.640
The ingredients are so seasonal, that as you
eat each bowl you can sense the season change

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.220
and the love that's put into it.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:12.160
The mixian noodles are made 100 percent out
of rice and water.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.930
They're slightly fermented, so they have a
hint of sourness to them.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.290
And because of the fermentation, when we cook
them they're also a little springy.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:25.380
It adds a good texture to the broth or sauce.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.060
The "Grandma Mixian" is inspired by Lijiang,
which is a very old town.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.350
We make a very wholesome broth that takes
about 24 to 36 hours to cook.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:40.440
It consists of chicken bones, duck bones,
and lots of different aromatics and spices.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.440
As it slowly simmers and evaporates down,
the broth gains more and more character.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.440
So, broth is a living thing.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:51.690
We confit chicken thighs in their own chicken
fat, so it's very tender and juicy.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:58.080
And then we also make our own house-fermented
chili, with fresh long hots, a little Thai

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.049
chili, garlic, and ginger all mixed together.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:05.130
We also make a black-garlic oil with black
sesame and roasted garlic, so it has a very

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.130
aromatic feel.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.480
It's very homey.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.519
We have cilantro, different kinds of seasonal
pickles, and then one of the most important

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:15.629
ingredients is the pu'er steeped-tea egg.

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:19.020
We steep the egg in tea, soy, and spice.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:24.470
In Yunnan, they grow different kinds of edible
flowers—they love flowers—so we added

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:29.000
some flowers and some perfume-y flavor to
the whole thing.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.360
So the flavor is pretty complex.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.920
I grew up loving mixian.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.890
I really understand and love the classic,
traditional kind of mixian found in China.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:47.859
But I come from an American fine-dining background,
so I went to a different part of Yunnan and

00:02:47.859 --> 00:02:49.709
tasted a different type of mixian.

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.950
I wanted to share the variety and the diversity
of Chinese cuisine.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.740
Chinese food is very-seasonally driven, because
we were all farmers and peasants.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.139
We would go to the market, we would buy ingredients,
we would stir-fry them, and we wouldn't have

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:04.139
much left over.

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:06.049
So it's very fresh and ingredient-driven.

00:03:06.049 --> 00:03:10.940
I want to do more as a chef in New York to
share that part of Chinese cooking, and to

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.239
tell a newer story about Chinese cuisine.

